{{- /* Drop ~/.keys on a machine with no age identity, so a keyless clone (or a
       brand-new box before the key is placed) applies cleanly instead of failing
       to decrypt. Once ~/.config/chezmoi/key.txt is in place, .keys applies. */}}
{{- if not (lstat (joinPath .chezmoi.homeDir ".config/chezmoi/key.txt")) }}
.keys
{{- end }}

{{- /* Desktop config is gated on HEADLESS, not on OS — a headless Linux box
       deserves the same treatment as FreeBSD.

       The signal is INSTALLED-COMPOSITOR PRESENCE, never a live $DISPLAY or
       $WAYLAND_DISPLAY. `chezmoi apply` run over ssh on a DESKTOP machine has no
       DISPLAY, and administering a desktop remotely is routine here, so a
       DISPLAY-keyed rule would decide that box is headless and stop managing its
       entire desktop config. Installed-compositor presence does not change between
       an ssh session and a local one on the same host, which is the property that
       makes it safe.

       This only stops files being APPLIED. chezmoi does NOT remove an already
       applied target for being ignored, so a box that later loses its compositor
       keeps its existing config, unmanaged. That is the accepted outcome: the
       alternative, listing these in .chezmoiremove, makes `chezmoi apply` fail
       outright with "inconsistent state" while the sources still exist. */}}
{{- if not (or (lookPath "Hyprland") (lookPath "sway") (lookPath "Xorg")) }}
.config/hypr
.config/sway
.config/waybar
.config/rofi
.config/wofi
.config/mako
.config/kitty
.config/ghostty
{{- end }}
